Hey AutoGen community! Built an open-source multi-agent orchestrator that solves a problem I kept hitting: **agents overwriting each other's work when running in parallel**.

## The problem

When you run multiple agents concurrently and they share state (a database, a file, an API), you get race conditions. Agent A reads a value, Agent B reads the same value, both write back — one update is silently lost. No error, no warning, just corrupted state.

## What Network-AI does

- **Per-key atomic commits** — propose → validate → commit pipeline with file-system mutexes. Two agents writing to different keys never block each other. Same key? Hash-based conflict detection catches it.
- **AuthGuardian** — weighted permission system (justification 40% + trust level 30% + risk 30%). Agents can't hallucinate permissions. Every grant gets a time-limited HMAC token.
- **Quality gate** — catches hallucinations, dangerous code (`eval`, `exec`, `rm -rf`), and placeholder content at ~159K-1M ops/sec before it hits shared state.
- **12 framework adapters** — AutoGen, LangChain, CrewAI, LlamaIndex, Semantic Kernel, OpenAI Assistants, Haystack, DSPy, Agno, MCP, OpenClaw, and custom. Plug in your existing agents, no rewrite needed.
- **Budget tracking** — token spend ceiling across parallel agents. Prevents runaway costs.

## Quick example

```typescript
import { createSwarmOrchestrator, CustomAdapter } from 'network-ai';

const adapter = new CustomAdapter();
adapter.registerHandler('analyst', async (payload) => {
  return { result: `Analysis complete: ${payload.action}` };
});

const orchestrator = createSwarmOrchestrator({
  adapters: [{ adapter }],
});

// Atomic write — no other agent can corrupt this
orchestrator.blackboard.write('report:q4', { status: 'done' }, 'analyst');[](url)[](url)

